Two years ago I decided to jump into Unity and C# head first.  I learned a ton of stuff doing tutorials and making stuff, but it wasn't clicking and I couldn't write my own code.  I was over my head.  Picked up GM Studio on sale a long time ago and never tried it because it wasn't a "real" game engine.
Saw GMS2 was out and upgraded for 40% off for owning the previous version.  I figured I would do a clean wipe and start all over with GMS2.  Did a couple of tutorials again using the drag and drop to just understand the how and why of programming.  I learned how to write basic code in Unity, the syntax, but not why or how the pieces all fit together - why some code went into one place and other code went somewhere else.
I switched over to real coding with GML (Gamemaker language) instead of drag and drop, but this time after doing a simple asteroids tutorial decided to fill it out into a real game.  That meant I would have to do stuff without 100% handholding.  I watched more tutorials about each system I wanted to add to get the general idea without copying the code, then went into GMS2 and added these systems my own way with an idea of how the logic works.  Then gears started turning in my head and stuff started clicking into place.  I finally get it.
if(hardHeaded) && (!understand)
{
instance_create_layer(x, y, "Instances", obj_get_GMS2;
}
else
{
draw_text(room_width/2, room_height/2 - 200, "You Win!");
game_end();
}
Edit - Oh yeah, the new version of Gamemaker Studio is very slick.  Not sure how much of an improvement it is over the previous version, but it's easy to work in, looks very nice, a real joy to use.  I don't know if I recommend GMS2 over the previous version, but I highly recommend it if you are serious about learning to program and how games work and need somewhere to start.
There are tons of resources on youtube and official forums to learn from.  Right up there with Unity with the sheer volume of knowledge and help available.

(Review writed with 2 hours gaming and all achievements unlocked - Dec 26th, 2015).
Morphopolis let you control of various insects over the course of five stages solving puzzles so that you may progress. You lead your bug down the paths, clicking on the eggs, flowers, and disembodied grasshopper legs hidden in the busy, detailed environments until you collect the prescribed number.
Pros:
- Looks great;
Cons:
- Strange/Slow gameplay;
- Few levels;
- Very easy to solve the puzzles;
- Boring challenges.
5,0/10.

I rarely review games, but this one is exceptional. Never mind the old negative reviews, as they were made way back when the game is still in Early Access, with lots of bugs. Now there are still bugs out there, but it has improved greatly.
UP points:
- Vast world (The first biome can easily as big as, if not bigger than, a Large map in Terraria);
- Various quests, both main and side;
- Interesting mechanism, both leveling and crafting;
- Good sound effects;
- Neat 2D graphic;
DOWN points:
- Bugs. Bugs! BUGS! There are a lot of bug fixes needed.
- The menu is not actually scaled with windows' dimensions (i.e. you cannot create new character in 800x600, in-game UI is partly of screen, etc.);
- The whole world is loaded in Unity so it will greatly drop you FPS on low-end PC;
- Character control is poorly built: the character's arm is like a stick attached to the body, it cannot bend so it is really awkward in close-ranged action;
- THERE IS NO HORIZONTAL INERTIA! There is vertical onertia, which mean it take time for you to speed up and slow down vertically. Horizontal: Nope. If you jetpack around and suddenly stop midair, you will drop straight down regardless of the initial speed.
The bugs are not game-breaking and are being quickly fixed by the developers, so the UP points outweigh the DOWN points. Worth every cents I paid for it.
